{"provider":"jkw-local","version":1,"sourceType":"DOCX","sourcePath":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user/65d8ea64-e382-5070-81e6-5dde97f8b555/scratchpad/corpus/fn2/docx/fn2_harvard_fullname_noindent_justified.docx","pdfPath":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user/65d8ea64-e382-5070-81e6-5dde97f8b555/scratchpad/corpus/fn2/docx/fn2_harvard_fullname_noindent_justified.pdf","generatedAt":"2026-11-02T06:38:33.401Z","text":"Introduction\nOsteoarthritis is a degenerative joint condition that leads to the gradual breakdown of articular\ncartilage and remodelling of the underlying bone. The patient described morning stiffness\nlasting less than thirty minutes, which is consistent with a mechanical rather than\ninflammatory process (Brophy & Fillingham, 2022).\nManagement should begin with education and a structured exercise programme, because\nstrengthening the quadriceps reduces pain and improves function for most patients. Weight\nreduction is also recommended when the body mass index exceeds the healthy range, and the\nclinician should revisit these goals at every follow-up visit.\nPharmacological treatment is reserved for symptoms that do not respond to conservative\nmeasures. Topical anti-inflammatory agents are preferred before oral agents in older adults,\nsince gastrointestinal and renal risks increase with age and with concurrent medication use.\nScreening recommendations must be reviewed at the same consultation. According to current\nguidance, adults aged forty-five to seventy-five should be offered colorectal cancer screening,\nand women aged forty to seventy-four should be offered biennial mammography unless a\nhigher-risk pathway applies.\nIn summary, the evidence supports a stepped approach that prioritises non-pharmacological\ncare, reserves medication for persistent symptoms, and integrates preventive screening into\nroutine visits so that the patient's long-term health is protected.\nReference List\nAl Halbusi, Hussam, Al-Sulaiti, Khalid Ibrahim and Alalwan, Ali Abdallah (2025) '